X:1
T:Bwmba
R:cân
S:https://amgueddfa.cymru/casgliadau/caneuongwerin/?id=15 (collected by D. Roy Saer)
N:Sung by John Thomas (born 1912), an engineer of Felin, Abercastell, near Mathri, north Pembrokeshire; recorded 10.8.63 (St Fagans tape 609). He learnt it at the age of 8-9, hearing his uncle Ben Phillips, "Ben Bach" (1871-1958), the well-known folk singer, sing it at local concerts; Ben Phillips recorded several folk songs for the BBC in 1952-3. A comic tirade against a worthless first wife, happily now departed, rich in local dialect; life with her and a happier second marriage lead to the advice in the last verse. Four of the verses (1, 2, 3 and 8), with practically the same tune, were printed in the Journal of the Welsh Folk Song Society (iv, 60) in 1951, from the Gwaun Valley near Fishguard.
N:In the chorus, the second half of the tune is sung to the last two lines of the verse ("Bwmba bwmba bwmba dwdl ei, / Bwmba bwmba beio," and then lines 4 and 5 again).
N:The quavers D in bar 1 and F in bar 8 (and the chorus's matching bar) are in brackets on the score: verse 1 needs them. Some crotchets (the G in bar 5, the second and fourth notes of bars 7 and 9, the second note of bar 8, and the same notes in the chorus) also have two small quavers on the same note printed with them, for verses with an extra syllable. Verse 9 starts A3 A | D3 D D2 F2 |.
